## The four ratchets

This project enforces four ratchets via CI (see
`.agentile/coverage/GATES.md`):

1. **Test count** — never decreases (Rule 3)
2. **Formal specs** — never decreases (Rule 10)
3. **CI tripwires** — append-only (Rules 5, 6, 11)
4. **Frontmatter coverage** — never decreases (Rule 12)

Baselines live in `.agentile/coverage/baseline.json`. Sprint close
updates them. CI fails the build on regression of any ratchet.

## When to use slash commands

Agentile ships with slash commands under `.claude/commands/`:

| Slash | What it does |
|-------|-------------|
| `/sprint kickoff <id> <slug>` | Create a new active sprint from template |
| `/sprint daily` | Append a daily log entry to the active sprint |
| `/sprint close` | Seed RETRO.md and print the close-out checklist |
| `/journal` | Start a new journal entry with proper frontmatter |
| `/essay` | Start a new essay with proper frontmatter |
| `/case-study` | Start a new case study with proper frontmatter |
| `/claim-grade` | Run the AI claim grader on a piece of text |
| `/audit-drive` | Walk through the audit-driven sprint workflow |
| `/ratchet-check` | Run all four ratchet checks locally |

Slash commands are convenience wrappers around `scripts/sprint.sh`
and `scripts/ci/check_*.py` — anything you can do via slash, you can
do via shell. The methodology runs without slash commands; they're
ergonomics.

## Commit + PR conventions

- Conventional commits with WP reference (Rule 4):
  `feat(WP-3.2): add ECVRF proposer election`
- PRs reference the sprint WP in the description (template at
  `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md` if the project ships one)
- Co-Authored-By footer when AI-assisted

